Z||000005||87ECO5||12-12-2000||03:48 PM||||OK - just out of curiosity ...<P>I've seen convertibles with the plastic windows that eventually discolor to the point where you can no longer see through them.<P>I'm assuming, to replace that window, you must replace the entire top.  Is this true?  If so, how much and where do you go to do this?||||reg||5
Z||000006||rockcrawl||12-12-2000||04:18 PM||||I'm using parts of a sunbird top for my Rossa Spyder. The top has a glass window and the donor car looked just like the one posted above. I think they were available either way.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||12-12-2000||05:59 PM||||Scott--Try JC Whitney for replacement rear windows and complete tops.  No idea what the price is, but they're there.  Right in the middle of the catalog.||||reg||1
Z||000008||wkayl||12-12-2000||08:52 PM||||Back in the old days (1977) i replaced the rear window on my 67 Buick Skylark conv.  In about 1988 or 89 I replaced the original top and another rear window.  Both times I used J.C Whitney parts.  I now need to replace them again.  Strong sun here in Colorado is hard on paint and tops. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||12-12-2000||08:58 PM||||I've never looked at the plastic rear windows up close, but I would be very surprised if the entire top had to be replaced. Early style MX-5s used plastic rear window and they are everywhere. Might be able to get an idea how the setup looks by looking at one in a parking lot.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cheever2||12-12-2000||09:15 PM||||Not much for comparison, but my old Scout SSII windows stayed clear for a very long time, and a new top was $150 from I.H.  That was the old days, as someone mentioned above, but it was also a much larger item than for a car.  <P>Sure miss my Scout.  Wish IH hadn't quit making them.  Guess I have a thing for discontinued & closeouts.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||WampusCat||12-12-2000||09:16 PM||||The rear window of 1st gen Miata's zip out.  Very easy to replace.  Don't know about cavaliers.||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||12-14-2000||07:45 PM||||I think it depended on if you order rear defrost window. You can replace just the plastic window, it sewn in at bottom, zipper at top and sides. Plastic isnt that bad, $140,000 Mercedes SL and Porsche all have plastic, I know I have a 560 SL and the top n plastic window is over 12 years old, and I drive it year round. Still all looks brand new.||||reg||1
Z||000013||william_thorniii||12-14-2000||08:38 PM||||87EC05,<P>Had an 86 Cav RS Convertable for a while with a plastic rear wdo.  As it aged, it turned severly brittle and yellowed.  After it cracked we replaced the wdo only.  It depends on the shop though.  I think that technically the windows are not replaceable.  We were told this by many shops, but the guys that were willing to sew in another one had no problems doing so.  <P>Not sure about the 91's though.  I will tell you that I don't ever remember seeing a Cav with a glass rear wdo.  This doesn't mean that they don't exist... you know how I HATE convertables so I just don't look at them much.  <P>Two convertable questions in as many days... are you and Lisa thinking of a convertable instead of a mini van now?  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
